            <ArticleTitle>The Serum Levels of Resistin and Its Relationship with Other Proinflammatory Cytokines in Patients with Alzheimer's Disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>59-63</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.9758/cpn.2017.15.1.59</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="Objective" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The present study aims to analyze the levels of resistin, tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-α), interleukin (IL)-1β, IL-6, IL-18, and C-reactive protein (CRP) in patients with Alzheimer's disease (AD) and also investigate a potential relationship between resistin levels and TNF-α, IL-1β, IL-6, IL-18, and CRP levels in patients with AD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Methods" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The study included fifty patients with AD and 30 healthy controls with normal cognitive functions. The serum resistin, TNF-α, IL-1β, IL-6, IL-18, and CRP levels were assessed. We performed a Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) to evaluate the general cognitive performance.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Results" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">The mean serum resistin, IL-1β, IL-18, and TNF-α levels were significantly higher in patients with AD compared with the controls (p=0.026, p=0.002, p=0.003, and p=0.038, respectively). The IL-6 and CRP levels did not differ between the groups (p=0.874 and p=0.941). The resistin levels were positively correlated with the levels of CRP and IL-18 (r=0.526, p＜0.001; r=0.402, p=0.004, respectively). MMSE scores and inflammatory markers were not correlated (p＞0.05 for all).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="Conclusion" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Serum resistin levels were significantly increased and correlated with some inflammatory markers in AD patients, suggesting that resistin might play a role in the inflammatory process of AD.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
